{"name":"Hemp Rope","hex":"#b9a379","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hemp","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tall annual herb, Cannabis sativa, native to Asia.","Various products of this plant, including fibres and the drug cannabis."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hemp"},{"term":"rope","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Thick strings, yarn, monofilaments, metal wires, or strands of other cordage that are twisted together to form a stronger line.","An individual length of such material."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To tie (something) with rope.","To throw a rope (or something similar, e.g. a lasso, cable, wire, etc.) around (something)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rope"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hemp","blocks":[{"text":"The term hemp is the vernacular English language word designating the plant Cannabis sativa, a plant that can be used to make a wide range of products, and be refined into a variety of commercial items, including paper, rope, textiles, clothing, biodegradable plastics, paint, insulation, biofuel, food, medicines, and animal feed. Along with bamboo, hemp is among the fastest growing plants on Earth and was also one of the first plants to be spun into usable fiber 50,000 years ago."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/La_Roche_Jagu_chanvre_1.JPG/330px-La_Roche_Jagu_chanvre_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hemp","wikidata":"Q7150699"},{"title":"Rope","blocks":[{"text":"A rope is a group of yarns, plies, fibres, or strands that are twisted or braided together into a larger and stronger form. Ropes have high tensile strength and can be used for dragging and lifting. Rope is thicker and stronger than similarly constructed cord, string, and twine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Cordage_en_chanvre.jpg/330px-Cordage_en_ch